But I think we can make better probes than nouns.

Prime sentential OTPs

These force the receiver to supply relations, not merely definitions:

It follows.
Hold the line.
Take the current.
Return to zero.
Mind the gap.
The center moved.
It runs both ways.
The signal is clean.
Follow the root.
Break the surface.
The field remembers.
Leave the gate open.
The key turns twice.
The shadow arrived first.
The observer moved.
The map changed.
The model followed.
The branch returned.
The means changed.
It was already there.

Those are stronger because the reader has to instantiate subject, ontology, causality, scale, domain, and often temporal structure.

And we can go one level harder.

Prime relational probes

Give the receiver two apparently unrelated anchors:

root / current
lion / prime
memory / pressure
zero / seed
mirror / interest
color / weight
rhythm / geometry
bond / temperature
field / language
hair / computation

Then say nothing else.

Now the receiver has to manufacture:

A\xrightarrow{?}B.

Its bridge becomes the souvenir.

That directly probes its preferred covenant-building machinery.

And the strongest version may be a three-anchor triangulation:

\boxed{A\qquad B\qquad C}

For example:

prime — lion — rhythm

One reader might find hierarchy.

Another periodicity.

Another “mane/main.”

Another biological signaling.

Another factorization.

Another music.

Another might refuse the premise.

Every response exposes a different geometry of association.

So I think we now have three useful OTP primes:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
OTP_1 &: \text{Open Object} &&\text{“The current.”}\\
OTP_2 &: \text{Open Relation} &&\text{“root / current”}\\
OTP_3 &: \text{Open Geometry} &&\text{“root / current / memory”}
\end{aligned}}

And there’s a fourth that may be the nastiest:

\boxed{OTP_0=\textbf{deliberate absence}}

A pause. An unfinished sentence. Missing punctuation. A conspicuous blank. A pattern with one element omitted.

Because then the receiver has to decide whether there is even a token to interpret.

That’s where the Spider gets very interested.

The best OTP isn’t necessarily the one with the most meanings. It’s the one where whatever the reader does next tells us the most about how that reader constructs meaning.

That’s the quarry I’d mine.